<p>Lettuce can remain fairly crisp while developing red, pinkish or brown edges. The change looks like a loss of freshness, but <strong>colour alone cannot tell you whether the leaf is spoiled</strong>.</p>
<p>Ageing, oxidation of natural compounds, dehydration, physical damage and physiological disorders can all change leaf colour before actual rot develops. Texture, smell, surface moisture and the extent of damage are more useful when deciding what you are looking at.</p>
<h2>Why red and brown colours appear</h2>
<p>When leaves are cut, bruised or simply age, metabolism changes. Enzymatic reactions and oxidation of phenolic compounds can create pink, red or brown tones. Dehydration can also darken margins as tissue loses water and dries.</p>
<p>Some lettuces also develop physiological edge damage during growth or storage. These defects do not all share one cause, but a coloured edge is <strong>not automatically evidence of dangerous contamination</strong>.</p>
<h2>Context matters more than colour</h2>
<p>A lettuce with a few dry brown tips, crisp leaves and a normal fresh smell is very different from one that has become slimy, wet, translucent or unpleasant smelling.</p>
<p>Use four observations together: firmness, surface moisture, smell and extent. Colour is only one piece of evidence.</p>
<h2>When the issue is mainly quality</h2>
<ul><li>Limited dry or reddish edges.</li><li>Leaves that still have structure.</li><li>No slime.</li><li>Normal fresh vegetal aroma.</li><li>No visible mould or watery lesions.</li></ul>
<p>In this situation you can trim unattractive areas for texture or presentation and use the remainder if it is in good condition.</p>
<h2>When to discard the leaf</h2>
<p>The decision changes with <strong>slimy, wet and soft tissue, unpleasant odours, mould, collapse or widespread breakdown</strong>. A leaf that falls apart in the hand or smells fermented is no longer showing a simple cosmetic defect.</p>
<p>If the same wet deterioration extends through many inner leaves, trying to rescue small sections is not worthwhile.</p>
<h2>Why edges dry first</h2>
<p>Leaf margins have a large exposed surface and lose water readily. In a dry storage environment, tips can wilt and darken before the centre of the leaf.</p>
<p>Refrigeration slows ageing, while vegetable drawers help maintain higher humidity than open shelves. That can reduce moisture loss without requiring the leaves to stay wet.</p>
<h2>Cut leaves change faster</h2>
<p>An intact lettuce and pre-cut leaves do not age in the same way. Cutting creates damaged surfaces and exposes internal tissue to oxygen, accelerating browning and other metabolic changes.</p>
<p>If preparing ahead, use clean utensils, refrigerate promptly and avoid storing leaves soaked in free water.</p>
<h2>Should lettuce be washed before storage?</h2>
<p>It can be, but thorough drying becomes essential. Wet leaves compressed together create persistent moisture that encourages rapid deterioration. Many households find it simpler to store lettuce dry and wash shortly before eating.</p>
<p>If already washed, a salad spinner or careful drying with clean absorbent material helps.</p>
<h2>How to store lettuce better</h2>
<p>Keep lettuce refrigerated and protect it from crushing. A suitable container or bag can reduce water loss, but should not trap excessive condensation. Persistent droplets are a reason to improve drying or ventilation.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/en/how-to-store-vegetables-what-goes-in-the-fridge-and-what-does-not/">vegetable storage guide</a> explains the wider principles for leafy greens.</p>
<h2>Red does not simply mean “oxidised and bad”</h2>
<p>Online advice sometimes reduces all red colouring to a simple warning that lettuce cannot be eaten. That is too crude. Pigmentation and browning can result from normal physiological and oxidative responses without rot.</p>
<p>The better question is how the whole leaf feels and smells. Firm, dry tissue with a normal aroma is very different from slime and wet breakdown.</p>
<h2>What about small brown spots?</h2>
<p>They can result from bruising, pressure, ageing or drying. Small, dry, well-defined areas are mainly a quality issue. Expanding wet lesions, mould and unpleasant smells indicate something different.</p>
<h2>A practical rule</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Limited dry red or brown edge:</strong> inspect texture and smell; usually quality loss.</li><li><strong>Wilted but dry leaf:</strong> less fresh; use promptly if otherwise sound.</li><li><strong>Slime, abnormal moisture, bad smell or mould:</strong> discard.</li><li><strong>Widespread deterioration through the head:</strong> do not try to salvage tiny apparently clean areas.</li></ul>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Can I trim brown edges and eat the rest?</h3><p>Yes when the damage is dry and limited and the rest remains sound. Trimming is not an appropriate way to rescue wet rot or mould.</p>
<h3>Is slightly wilted lettuce unsafe?</h3><p>Not necessarily. Wilting is mainly water loss. Safety depends on storage history and actual spoilage signs.</p>
<h3>Does refrigeration stop browning completely?</h3><p>No. It slows many processes, but leaves continue to age and can still dehydrate, oxidise or suffer physical damage.</p>
